# Original exact derivation before candidate exposure

Input supplied by the parent: H=3I+XX+YY+ZZ, C=2(I+YY)+8(I+ZZ), and B=(X+2Y+3Z) tensor I. The parent is seeking an escape from the previously exposed first-site-only static-bias counterexample. That purpose and these equations are known. No file in heisenberg_edge_bias_candidate has been read before this original derivation and its seal. This is independent recomputation from the equations by the same model, not blind selection of the example or different-model-family replication.

Let the computational basis be |00>,|01>,|10>,|11>, and choose the following Bell basis in increasing-energy order for every lambda>0:

    e0=Psi-=(|01>-|10>)/sqrt(2),
    e1=Psi+=(|01>+|10>)/sqrt(2),
    e2=Phi+=(|00>+|11>)/sqrt(2),
    e3=Phi-=(|00>-|11>)/sqrt(2).

Since XX, YY and ZZ commute, direct Pauli multiplication diagonalizes

    H+lambda C=(3+10lambda)I+XX+(1+2lambda)YY+(1+8lambda)ZZ.

Their eigenvalues in this basis are exactly

    E0=0,
    E1=4+4lambda,
    E2=4+16lambda,
    E3=4+20lambda.

For lambda>0 these are strictly increasing. The six positive gaps, labeled by ordered pairs a>b, are

| Pair | Gap |
|---|---|
| 10 | 4+4lambda |
| 20 | 4+16lambda |
| 30 | 4+20lambda |
| 21 | 12lambda |
| 31 | 16lambda |
| 32 | 4lambda |

Within either the first three or last three rows the gaps are distinct for lambda>0. Comparing across the two groups gives only two positive collisions: lambda=1/3 makes Delta10=Delta31, and lambda=1/2 makes Delta10=Delta21. All other cross-group equality solutions are negative or absent. Thus the simple-spectrum/distinct-positive-gap conditions hold precisely for lambda>0 excluding {1/3,1/2}. At the excluded positive values the energies remain simple but gaps repeat. At lambda=0 the triplet degeneracy returns.

Direct application of the local Pauli operators gives the energy-basis port matrix

    B_E = [[ 0,   3, -2i, -1],
           [ 3,   0,   1, 2i],
           [ 2i,  1,   0,  3],
           [-1, -2i,   3,  0]].

Every off-diagonal entry is nonzero, with magnitudes 1,2 or 3. The diagonal entries vanish; the qualified bus hypothesis does not require nonzero diagonals. The Bell eigenbasis is independent of lambda, so this support statement holds at every lambda and in particular at every positive strong point. The exact identity B^2=14I gives ||B||=sqrt(14).

At lambda=1 the energies are (0,8,20,24) and the six positive gaps are {4,8,12,16,20,24}, all distinct. This supplies a concrete computable strong point H+C with a local first-qubit B. More strongly, every positive lambda<1/3 is strong, so arbitrarily small rational choices need no polynomial-root search in this explicit example.

The actual positive bias decomposition is already displayed. The block 2(I+YY) has eigenvalues 0 and 4, each twice; the block 8(I+ZZ) has eigenvalues 0 and 16, each twice. Their sum C has eigenvalues (0,4,16,20) in the Bell basis and norm 20. Both blocks are supported on the same existing old nearest-neighbor edge. At each endpoint their combined added incident norm is 4+16=20, or 20lambda after attenuation. Combining them into one positive edge block has the same norm 20. H itself has eigenvalues 0,4,4,4 and norm 4, so the total old two-qubit generator has norm 4+20lambda for lambda>0. All original H terms remain installed with coefficient one; the additions change the YY and ZZ edge coefficients by 2lambda and 8lambda.

For the transfer theorem's ledger, take the given C as the unreserved bias and its two displayed positive terms as C_alpha, with r_alpha=0. Then R_C=0 and J_C=20. The identity component 10I is an actual part of C: its normalized trace is s_C=10, and the spectral midpoint and centered norm are c_C=10 and a_C=10. It is not an omitted reserve. An alternative convention that calls 2YY+8ZZ the unreserved bias and 10I the reserve changes the nominal drift by a scalar and must change the output phase consistently; the two conventions must not be silently conflated.

This supplies the qualified static-bias transfer's strong-point input **only if adding and attenuating these two-qubit interactions on the existing old edge is admissible**. It is outside the earlier permission restricted to Hermitian bias on the first old qubit alone. The single-site obstruction therefore remains true in its original domain. Access to a first-site external port does not by itself grant independent YY/ZZ edge additions. B remains a local first-site operator; the new access is in C.

For the positive transfer one may choose a positive rational lambda<1/3 small enough that 10lambda w<=epsilon/16 (or use the theorem's conservative computable-upper-bound recipe). The old drift through the full returned time is compensated by its post-T interaction target; no lambda T smallness is needed. The existing conditional theorem then supplies its complete-output, actual-time finite-window conclusion, provided its geometry, computable target, controller-state preparation, timing, isolation and full-generator calibration premises are supplied. This four-dimensional example assumes the complete old plant is exactly these two qubits; arbitrary additional old apparatus/filler factors are not silently omitted or proven to satisfy the same spectrum conditions.

Using h=4, c_bound=20, J_C=20 and the theorem's sufficient R_s>=24 gives the same form of fixed controller incident cap, J_*=20+R_s+7M_E+2/t_*, with one finite M_E from its uniform outer energy bounds. The example does not provide a native supplier for the added edge controls, coherent sources or calibration, and gives no favorable runtime or precision bound. It is an explicit escape under stronger old-edge access, not a derivation of that permission from the original first-site-only model.

An actual exact matrix computation accompanies this derivation. The source and launcher are captured before execution, and stdout/stderr and exits are retained. The original seal precedes candidate inspection and carries no audit or publication status.
